      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "To solve $x^2 - 3x + 2 = 0$ graphically, you would find where the graph of $y = x^2 - 3x + 2$:",
      "options": [
        { "id": "a", "text": "Crosses the $y$-axis" },
        { "id": "b", "text": "Has its maximum point" },
        { "id": "c", "text": "Crosses the $x$-axis" },
        { "id": "d", "text": "Crosses the line $y = 2$" }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "Setting $y = 0$ gives $x^2 - 3x + 2 = 0$. The solutions are the $x$-coordinates where the graph crosses the $x$-axis."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "The graphs of $y = x^2$ and $y = 2x + 3$ intersect. The $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are the solutions of:",
      "options": [
        { "id": "a", "text": "$x^2 + 2x + 3 = 0$" },
        { "id": "b", "text": "$x^2 - 2x - 3 = 0$" },
        { "id": "c", "text": "$x^2 - 2x + 3 = 0$" },
        { "id": "d", "text": "$x^2 + 2x - 3 = 0$" }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "b",
      "explanation": "At intersection points, $x^2 = 2x + 3$. Rearranging: $x^2 - 2x - 3 = 0$."
    }
  ]
